Key observations:

1. `NBD_LFS 1` — meaning `_FILE_OFFSET_BITS 64` gets defined via lfs.h. nbdsrv.c includes lfs.h via nbdsrv.h (which starts with `#include "lfs.h"`). But how do the angle-bracket includes `<nbdsrv.h>`/`<cliserv.h>`/`<treefiles.h>` get resolved? DEFAULT_INCLUDES = -I. — so angle-bracket includes also search the -I directories, i.e., the current (build=source) directory. OK, so `<treefiles.h>` resolves to /workspace/nbd/treefiles.h. ✓

2. off_t consistency: treefiles.c includes "config.h" but does not include lfs.h. Without `_FILE_OFFSET_BITS=64`, on this platform off_t would be 32-bit in treefiles.c, whereas nbdsrv.c/nbd-server.c get 64-bit off_t via lfs.h. This is an ABI mismatch: struct/stat calls, and the off_t argument passed to construct_path/open_treefile, would be misinterpreted!

Wait — but on x86_64 Linux, is off_t still 32-bit by default without _FILE_OFFSET_BITS=64? Actually it is! On x86_64, off_t is `long` = 64-bit regardless. _FILE_OFFSET_BITS mainly affects 32-bit platforms. On LP64 Linux, off_t is always 64-bit. So no ABI mismatch on this platform. But the upstream treefiles.c — does it include lfs.h? The upstream version I fetched does not include lfs.h. And upstream nbd is built for 32-bit too… cliserv.h does the following:

```c
#if NBD_LFS==1
#ifndef _LARGEFILE_SOURCE
#define _LARGEFILE_SOURCE
#endif
#define _FILE_OFFSET_BITS 64
#endif
```

And treefiles.c includes cliserv.h! So _FILE_OFFSET_BITS gets defined via cliserv.h before the inclusion of unistd.h/sys/stat.h… wait, the order in my treefiles.c: fcntl.h, limits.h, inttypes.h, stdio.h, unistd.h, sys/stat.h, sys/types.h come before config.h/cliserv.h. Hmm! So fcntl.h/unistd.h/sys/stat.h get included before _FILE_OFFSET_BITS is defined by cliserv.h. On 32-bit platforms this would be a problem; on x86_64 it's fine. Upstream has exactly this include order (the fetched content shows fcntl.h...sys/types.h, then config.h, cliserv.h, treefiles.h, nbd-debug.h). Hmm, wait, let me actually double-check the fetched order:

```c
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <limits.h> // for PATH_MAX
#include <inttypes.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/stat.h>
#include <sys/types.h>

#include "config.h"
#include "cliserv.h"
#include "treefiles.h"
#include "nbd-debug.h"
```

Yeah — the system headers come first. This is upstream's exact order. cliserv.h's comment says "this defines behavior related to the following headers, so it must come before them" — but treefiles.c doesn't follow that. In any case: it works on x86_64; and for fidelity, match upstream.

Actually, wait — there's one more subtle point. glibc headers are guarded by include-once, but the declarations of open/lseek etc. depend on _FILE_OFFSET_BITS at the point of first inclusion. In treefiles.c, fcntl.h is included before _FILE_OFFSET_BITS is defined → open = open64? On x86_64 there is no distinction. Not an issue here.

3. One more thing to check: my treefiles.c uses `err()` → `nbd_err()`. nbd_err in cliserv.c — let's check whether it uses MY_NAME or syslog. In nbd-server, cliserv.c is compiled with ISSERVER defined (the msg() macro in nbdsrv.h uses syslog if ISSERVER). Let's verify how nbd_err behaves in the server build — actually not important, it's proven in upstream.

